    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-12740",
      "problem": "The shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x)?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/6f06759c9aa4f88b73460ed28b9354026bded56aa368ae0c56645ad2ab890bd6.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Since $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x) \\equiv \\neg P(x) \\lor Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set $\\overline{T}_P{:}$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/1fb4c7a43fde2587d01e810be898fba7c4a4acf066851ee636b8bc41cdfe7f0d.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $\\neg P(x) \\lor Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to $\\overline{T}_p$ or $T_Q{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/30eddda009ba76789427d5b76e9ed5aeb709dd75509fabd06e2a71c03483de70.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14253769/assets/3e44b650b220edc54c55a6407d3d8f221621fdd2109019cba04541239904eabf.png)",
